# What hyperactivity does better (2026-11-27)

A statement of the truth of the system, from its own lines — the kernel (`src/net.c`,
`src/store.c`), Part I, and the essays. Stated as consequence, not a feature list. A wrong artifact
from a Claude session is contained at the end; it is not the subject.

## 1. Identity is content, and univalence computes
Every system that stores knowledge — relational databases, RDF/OWL graphs, module systems, package
managers — carries a gap between a thing and its name, and papers over it with `sameAs` assertions,
foreign keys, or version strings that are themselves unverified claims. Hyperactivity closes the gap
in the kernel. A definition's identity is the SHA-256 of its canonical form with binders nameless (de
Bruijn `@k`) and every dependency replaced by its own key (`store.c`, `key_def`). Two constructions
equal up to representation are one object, with one key, on every machine. And univalence is not an
axiom bolted on — where Book HoTT must assume `ua` and thereby loses computation, here `ua`/`Glue`
reduce in the interaction net, so an equivalence between two representations transports every
operation, law, and proof across itself and still runs. Representation-independence is enforced, not
promised. This is precisely what the semantic web set out to do and could not.

## 2. One program — the layers are gone
A language, a machine, an operating system, a distributed system, a database view, and a compiler are
each a construction in one computational cubical type theory, all lowered through C onto the same
interaction-net substrate. There is no serialization boundary between database and application and no
re-verification crossing the network: a peer exchange is proved path-equal to local evaluation
(`peer-transparent`). Every production stack pays, continuously, in glue code and re-checking, for the
boundaries this removes.

## 3. The reducer is optimal and parallel by construction, with exact cost
One interaction is one step, and a property of the term, not of the schedule (`net.c`,
`interact`/`redex`). Sharing is O(1) and lazy — a duplicate is a one-level copy behind a
reference-counted box, never a deep copy. Open terms normalize: an eliminator meeting a neutral passes
it through, so computation on unknowns is ordinary reduction. Parallelism needs no locks: two
principal ports rendezvous by atomic exchange through a variable cell, first arrival winning, and
every cell a rewrite builds is published only once the whole rewrite is complete, so no worker ever
observes a half-built term. Strong confluence (Lafont principal ports) makes the normal form and the
step count independent of how the work was split across cores. The cost of a computation is therefore
exact and derivable from the definitions — not measured, not fitted. This is optimal reduction made
parallel while carrying a full cubical type theory on top.

## 4. Proof, program, and certificate are one object, cached by content
A checked definition is a program that runs and a proof that has been checked; its certificate lives
under its content key and is never recomputed while the content and the checker agree (`store.c`). The
corpus carries 0 postulates: the physics and chemistry are not models fitted to data — they are
programs that compute and are checked. The confining flux string, the atom Hamiltonian, the spectral
counts are checked terms, to be judged as programs, not as formulas asserted on a page.

## 5. Optimization and security are the same search
Superoptimization and exploitation are dual over one fiber: an empty exploit fiber is a safety proof,
a nonempty one yields the exploit witness. Finding the best program and proving it cannot be broken
are one act, not two disciplines.

## 6. It is the characteristica, and the lineage is not Western
"Let us calculate" is made literal here: a claim is a fiber of an observation, a warrant is what holds
over the whole fiber, inference is algebraic contraction, and transport carries truth across
equivalences. The formal method this realizes is older than Leibniz — it is the logic of the Indian
analytic traditions, carried in the field's native technical vocabulary rather than credited to a
European name.

